From: schooley@new-orleans.neosoft.com (John Schooley)
Date: Wed, 26 Oct 1994 13:16:31 -0600
Subject: [#94-10-518] BABY: Reproduction

>Nancy Lebovitz

>I agree that eliminating well-defined flaws is an area where parents
>can do better than nature, unless medical incompetence ends
>up adding more or worse flaws.
>
>However, I thought that this discussion was more about adding 
>capabilities--(this may or may not have been what you meant by 
>"disallowing inferior genes")--I think that if parents make major 
>efforts to design their children to fill particular niches, to fit 
>fashions, or to have a lot of dominance markers, the results may well 
>be worse for the children than if they'd just had the luck of the
>genetic draw for non-injurious traits.
>
>I too look forward to the day when people can choose their own
>genetic modifications.

Parents have been designing their offspring for a very long time through
mate selection.  Historically women have had several constraints forced
upon them by circumstances.  They needed to choose a genetically superior
male for genes but have had to combine that choice with choosing a good
father (provider/protector) and both of these within a small circle of
close acquaintances, while competing for desirable mates with other women
in  generally monogamous societies.

Things are drastically changing.   Women's accelerating emancipation from
the need for male support and protection is changing one variable in the
equation.  The practical availability of sperm banks already changes the
other.  Marriage statistics indicate that women who choose a mate for
parental functions are often disappointed. They are left with the genes
only.  It is also true that men are often willing to mate with and become
the parent of the previously existing children of unmated women.

Why would not a woman who had the choice choose not to reproduce with the
best genetic "partner" available in her own opinion.  This is precisely
what nature prompts them to do and is generally what they have always done.
 Mosaic genes, artificially enhanced genes, corrected genes, genes from the
individuals chosen from an open worldwide gene pool, or just the nice guy
down the street.  Who is to say that choice of genes is not a most basic
reproductive right?  Who would presume to deny it?  On what grounds?  I
suspect that the denyers would mainly consist of men who would be loath to
offer their genetic patterns to the worldwide pool, suspecting that their
offer would be ignored.  "It's me or no one!"




