Vigdor --

I would be happy to discuss this issue with you, but this is not the
appropriate forum for it.  If you wish to have meaningful dialogue on this
subject with those who hold an extropian viewpoint, I suggest the
following:

0) Read the list rules and the Extropian Principles.  Proposing an enormous
tax-funded government initiative, and expecting members of this forum to
take it seriously, was simply bad manners.

1) Read, and be able to discuss, a significant subset of Julian Simon's
writings on the subject.  Any of his books _The Resourceful Earth_,
_Population Matters_, and _The Ultimate Resource_ (all reviewed in a recent
issue of _Extropy_) would provide an excellent start.

2) When you know a little bit about what Simon's research indicates about
the environmental debate, particularly the vested interests of
disasturbators like Ehrlich and Rifkin in promoting environmental hysteria,
formulate your critique and post it to the alt.extropians newsgroup, which
exists just for this type of abecedarian discussion.

3) E-mail me, and any other list members who have expressed an interest in
this topic, letting us know that you have posted your critique to
alt.extropians.  We will read it and reply there.  DO NOT simply post your
response to Simon's work to this list; that would be a flagrant display of
further bad manners -- an initial lapse could be excused by some as the
result of exuberance and inexperience, but to continue confronting list
members with information they have told you they consider irrelevant would
be provocatively offensive.

4) Confine further debate on environmental apocalypse theory to the
alt.extropians newsgroup.

Thank you for your attention and cooperation.
